    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d655">

                <pb facs="0844" n="834" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg834"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.243/5–2647</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Chinese
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Ringwalt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1947-05-28">May 28, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Responsive to Mr. Vincent’s memorandum of May 26, 1947 in regard to the transfer
                    of munitions to China, the following action has been taken: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">“Generalissimo” rifle ammunition</hi>. Dr.
                            Shao-Hwa Tan, Minister of the Chinese Embassy, and Mr. L. F. Chen of the
                            Chinese Supply Commission have been informed that there is no objection
                            to the purchase of this ammunition through OFLC channels. Colonel Muir,
                            P &amp; O, War Department, and Mr. Upton, OFLC, have been given the
                            appropriate authorization to proceed with this transfer. (See first
                            paragraph of the attached memorandum of May 26 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >28</hi>], 1947, addressed to Colonel Muir.<anchor n="2"
                                xml:id="d655-f1"/>)</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">8</hi>⅓ <hi rendition="#italic">Air Group
                                Program</hi>. Colonel Muir and Mr. Upton have been authorized to
                            include in the items being transferred to the Chinese Government under
                            this Program transport planes and spare parts for all equipment
                            transferred thereunder. (See second paragraph of attached memorandum of
                            May 26 [<hi rendition="#italic">28</hi>], 1947.) </item>
                        <pb facs="0845" n="835" xml:id="pg835"/>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Policy Committee on Arms Control</hi>. The
                            necessary clearance for the transfer of the “Generalissimo” rifle
                            ammunition has been obtained from the PCA<anchor n="3" xml:id="d655-f2"
                            /> by the FE member in consultation with General Crain of A–H.<anchor
                                n="4" xml:id="d655-f3"/> In view of the policy established by the
                            PCA on July 29, 1946, of withholding arms and ammunition from the
                            Chinese Government, the FE member will report to the PCA at its next
                            meeting with the recommendation that this restriction on the shipment of
                            arms to China be rescinded.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">British and Canadian Governments</hi>. On May
                            26, 1947, Mr. H. A. Graves, Counselor of the British Embassy, and Mr.
                            Ralph Collins, Second Secretary of the Canadian Embassy, were orally
                            informed that this Government is now prepared to grant export licenses
                            covering shipments of arms to China.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Arthur</hi> R. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Ringwalt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
